WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.750
llama.

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.778
cpp can be used as a local inference runtime and, at a high level,

00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:09.369
as a server that exposes model inference through API endpoints.

00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:11.883
API serving lets web UIs, scripts, applications,

00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:16.552
and automation call the model without manually running each prompt from a terminal.

00:00:16.552 --> 00:00:21.580
This turns a local model into a service boundary that other tools can use.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.890
Many local AI tools expect OpenAI-compatible API patterns at a high level.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.686
Compatibility can make integration easier,

00:00:27.686 --> 00:00:31.995
but it does not remove the need to understand the local service.

00:00:31.995 --> 00:00:37.023
Operators still need to know which model is loaded, what context length is configured,

00:00:37.023 --> 00:00:39.896
what endpoint is reachable, how requests are logged,

00:00:39.896 --> 00:00:42.770
and whether streaming or structured output is supported.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.565
Network exposure should be conservative.

00:00:44.565 --> 00:00:48.875
Local-only binding is a safer default for a private inference server because

00:00:48.875 --> 00:00:52.826
it reduces the chance that unintended users can reach the endpoint.

00:00:52.826 --> 00:00:57.494
LAN access, reverse proxies, authentication, and TLS may be useful in shared environments,

00:00:57.494 --> 00:00:59.290
but they should be deliberate.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:02.163
Avoid accidental public exposure of private inference services.

00:01:02.163 --> 00:01:05.755
Prompts and outputs should be treated as potentially sensitive data.

00:01:05.755 --> 00:01:09.705
They may include personal notes, source code, documents, secrets, business information,

00:01:09.705 --> 00:01:10.783
or operational details.

00:01:10.783 --> 00:01:15.092
If an API server is connected to a web UI or application,

00:01:15.092 --> 00:01:19.043
think about logging, retention, access control, and who can send requests.

00:01:19.043 --> 00:01:22.275
Serving a model is not just a convenience feature.

00:01:22.275 --> 00:01:23.780
It is an operational decision.
